---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site';

const title = "Security & Threat Model — VibeShield";
const description = "VibeShield security architecture, local offline execution model, trust boundaries, and responsible vulnerability disclosure policy.";
---

<LegalLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="/security"
  eyebrow="Trust & Architecture"
  h1="Security & Threat Model"
  updated={siteConfig.legalLastUpdated}
  summary="VibeShield is built on a zero-trust, zero-egress architecture. All code parsing and rule evaluation occurs 100% locally in-process or on your CI runner. No source code, secrets, or file contents leave your machine."
>
  <section id="what-leaves-your-machine">
    <h2>1. What Leaves Your Machine</h2>
    <p>
      The core security promise of VibeShield is absolute data containment:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Zero Egress on Every Surface:</strong> The CLI binary, the npm launcher, the pre-commit hook, and the IDE integrations execute rule matching exclusively in local memory. There is no telemetry, no cloud logging, no user tracking, and no external code transfer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No Backend Server:</strong> VibeShield maintains no centralized SaaS backend, databases, or cloud infrastructure. The compiled Go binary contains zero HTTP networking client code for code egress.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>CI/CD Runner Isolation:</strong> In GitHub Action mode, the action runs entirely inside your self-hosted or GitHub-hosted runner. It communicates only with GitHub's official API using your repository's native <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> to post findings directly to the pull request.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No AI Model Training:</strong> Because your source code and prompts are never collected or stored, nothing is ever used for model training or fine-tuning.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="trust-boundaries">
    <h2>2. Trust Boundaries & Execution Isolation</h2>
    <p>
      The table below details where each component runs, what data it accesses, and its network posture:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Component</th>
          <th scope="col">Execution Environment</th>
          <th scope="col">Data Access &amp; Boundaries</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Scanner (Go Binary)</strong></td>
          <td>Local machine or CI runner</td>
          <td>Read-only filesystem access to target repository. No outbound network connections linked.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Rule Packs (YAML)</strong></td>
          <td>In-process memory</td>
          <td>Validated against strict schema at load using RE2 regexes. Malformed packs terminate execution immediately with exit code 2.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>npm Launcher</strong></td>
          <td>Local Node.js environment</td>
          <td>Downloads verified release binary and validates its SHA-256 hash against <code>sha256sums.txt</code> before execution.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>GitHub Action</strong></td>
          <td>GitHub Actions runner</td>
          <td>Reads git diff and posts PR comment. Operates solely within repository permission scope.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="how-scanner-reads-code">
    <h2>3. How the Scanner Reads Your Code</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Read-Only Access:</strong> The scanner never modifies your source files during <code>scan</code>. Automated remediation via <code>fix</code> requires explicit interactive confirmation or preview.</li>
      <li><strong>No Execution of Scanned Code:</strong> Scanned files are treated strictly as text and AST trees; VibeShield never executes, builds, or runs tests on the inspected code.</li>
      <li><strong>Automatic Secret Redaction:</strong> When a suspected secret (such as an API token or private key) is detected in a diff, the finding redacts all but four prefix and suffix characters before outputting to terminal or PR comments.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="disclosure">
    <h2>4. Coordinated Vulnerability Disclosure</h2>
    <p>
      If you discover a security vulnerability or bypass in VibeShield, we ask that you report it responsibly and coordinate disclosure with us before public release.
    </p>
    <h3>How to Report</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Preferred Method:</strong> Open a private security report via
        <a href={siteConfig.social.securityAdvisories} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Security Advisories</a>.
        This provides an encrypted, private channel accessible only to maintainers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Direct Security Email:</strong> Alternatively, contact our security team at
        <code>{siteConfig.securityEmail}</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Security.txt:</strong> Machine-readable disclosure information is published at
        <code>/.well-known/security.txt</code> in compliance with RFC 9116.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Response Targets</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Phase</th>
          <th scope="col">Target Response Time</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Initial Acknowledgement</td>
          <td>Within 48 hours</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Triage &amp; Severity Assessment</td>
          <td>Within 5 business days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Patch Release (High / Critical)</td>
          <td>Within 14 calendar days</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Public Release &amp; Attribution</td>
          <td>Coordinated following patch deployment</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      We provide public attribution in our release notes and changelog for security researchers who report in good faith.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="quality-precision">
    <h2>5. Detection Precision & Benchmark Integrity</h2>
    <p>
      Security scanners are only useful if developers trust their output without fatigue. Our release criteria mandate:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>&ge; 85% precision on Critical and High severity findings evaluated against our curated golden test suite.</li>
      <li>&le; 5 false positives per 1,000 scanned diff lines.</li>
      <li>Auditable dismiss rationale requiring developer justification when dismissing rule findings.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
